  "statement": "An old lab calculator takes its input in postfix order: operands first, then the operator.\n\nGiven a list of string `tokens`, where each token is an integer or one of `+ - * /`, evaluate the expression and return the integer result. Division **truncates toward zero** (so `-7 / 2` is `-3`). The input is always a valid expression and never divides by zero.",
  "function": {
    "name": "eval_postfix",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "tokens",
        "type": "list[str]"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(tokens) <= 10^4",
    "intermediate values fit in a 32-bit signed integer"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [
        [
          "3",
          "4",
          "+",
          "2",
          "*"
        ]
      ],
      "expected": 14,
      "explanation": "(3 + 4) * 2 = 14."
    },
    {
      "args": [
        [
          "10",
          "3",
          "/"
        ]
      ],
      "expected": 3,
      "explanation": "10 / 3 truncates to 3."
    }
  ],
  "starter": {
    "python": "def eval_postfix(tokens):\n    # Write your solution here\n    pass\n"
  },
  "visibleTests": [
    {
      "args": [
        [
          "5"
        ]
      ],
      "expected": 5
    },
    {
      "args": [
        [
          "2",
          "7",
          "-"
        ]
      ],
      "expected": -5
    }
  ],
